<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaret III, Countess of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1350–1405</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Margaret of Male, Margaret of Flanders</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaret III, Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, known as Margaret of Male, was the heiress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-count-of-flanders">Louis of Male</a>, count of Flanders. Her marriage in 1369 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, brought Flanders, Artois, Nevers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a> and the Free County of Burgundy to the Valois dukes on her father's death in 1384. The union laid the foundations of the Burgundian State in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-iii-countess-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Margaret III, Countess of Flanders" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flemish_School_-_Lille_-_Margaret_of_Dampierre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
